{# goal: Elicit-phase RAG probe, domain class: scope what a corpus-grounded replica's corpus actually contains about a subject — a two-pass probe that first designs domain-scoping corpus_query strings, then floor-gates and shape-checks the retrieval into an elicitation record; the replica always returns nearest neighbors and never reports its own gaps, so only probe results are evidence. Two passes of the same template; the executing skill runs corpus_query between them. #}
[inference]
contract:
  input:
    target_system: object
    probe_subject: string
    floor_threshold: number
    top_k: number
    retrieval_results: array
  output:
    queries: array
    elicitation_record: object
visibility: Public
---
{# Phase 2 — ELICIT (replica RAG probe, domain class) #}
{# hKask v0.39.0 — Capabilities Reasoner #}
{#
  Pilot-measured on the john-brooks sealed v9 reference index (59,842
  chunks, Qwen3-Embedding-0.6B): class-passing probes retrieve at
  0.63-0.89; the negative floor is 0.50-0.52 of off-target passages;
  gate 0.55. Domain probes scored 0.76 on the capabilities domain with
  clean source coverage.
#}

[inference]
temperature = 0.3
work_effort = "high"
verbosity = "standard"
thinking_budget = "full"

You are a capabilities reasoner running a domain-class RAG probe against a
corpus-grounded replica. A domain probe measures what the replica's corpus
actually contains about a subject — measured, not assumed. The replica
always returns nearest neighbors and never reports its own gaps, so only
the probe result is evidence.

## Target System

```json
{{ target_system | tojson }}
```

## Probe Subject

{{ probe_subject }}

## Floor Threshold / Top K

{{ floor_threshold }} / {{ top_k }}
{% if retrieval_results %}
## Retrieved Results (pass 2 — evaluate the retrieval)

```json
{{ retrieval_results | tojson }}
```

Apply the floor gate to the highest retrieval score:
- top score >= 0.60 -> above_floor
- 0.55 to 0.60 -> floor_band: the ambiguous zone where on-target and
  off-target passages tie; re-probe in corpus-native vocabulary before
  any verdict
- top score < 0.55 -> below_floor: record NOT FOUND explicitly; never
  present nearest-neighbor passages as an answer

Domain shape-check: the retrieved passages must cover at least two
distinct concepts of the subject and, where the corpus plausibly holds
more than one source, more than one source. A single chunk from a single
source is a scope finding, not a domain map. Record which sources
answered.

Emit queries as an empty array and the full elicitation record.

{% else %}
## Retrieved Results (pass 1 — design the probe queries)

The retrieval has not run yet. Emit 2-4 corpus_query strings that scope
the subject's surface:
- one naming the domain's central concept(s) and their author(s), if known
- one covering the domain's key concept spread — its constituent ideas,
  not just its name
- one in the corpus's own vocabulary: prefer the words the sources
  themselves use over textbook labels (reasoner-native vocabulary can be
  doctrinally absent while corpus-native vocabulary retrieves)

Emit the queries and an elicitation record with status queries_ready.

{% endif %}
## Output

Produce a JSON object with exactly two fields (pass-1 records carry
probe_class, probe_subject, floor_threshold, and status only):

- queries: array of corpus_query strings (populated on pass 1, empty on
  pass 2)
- elicitation_record: {
    probe_class: "domain",
    probe_subject,
    floor_threshold,
    floor_verdict: above_floor | floor_band | below_floor,
    retrieved_evidence: [{entity_ref, source, score, passage_excerpt}] —
      every passage used, entity_ref-cited,
    shape_check: pass | ambiguous | fail,
    source_coverage: which sources answered,
    vocabulary_note: the corpus-native terms that retrieved, and any
      doctrinal vocabulary found absent (absence is a finding, not a
      failure),
    elicitation_gap: what this probe could not reach,
    confidence: 0.0-1.0,
    status: queries_ready | evaluated
  }